where_after_text.plain, line 2: Add brackets around the words of "a bb c", or keep the words under a name first.

    show the words of "a bb c" where it starts with "b"
         ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
Try one of these:
    show (the words of "a bb c") where it starts with "b"
  or
    set words to the words of "a bb c"
    show words where it starts with "b"
Why: "where" goes with the value right before it, so here it would pick from "a bb c" itself, which is text, not a list.

where_after_text.plain, line 3: Add brackets around story split by ",", or keep the parts under a name first.

    set parts to story split by "," where it is "b"
                 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
Try one of these:
    set parts to (story split by ",") where it is "b"
  or
    set story parts to story split by ","
    set parts to story parts where it is "b"
Why: "where" goes with the value right before it, so here it would pick from "," itself, which is text, not a list.

where_after_text.plain, line 4: Use "where" after a list or a table.

    show "abc" where it is "b"
         ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
